\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

\subsection{Architectures and Benchmark}
\label{sec:setup_overview}

We study two VLA architectures whose vision connectors differ in how they map encoder patches to language model input tokens (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (451.5M parameters) encodes each camera image with SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} into 1024 patches on a $32 \times 32$ grid, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4 \times 4$ block into one token by channel concatenation, producing 64 tokens per camera (128 total from two cameras). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4 \times 4$ receptive field with no redundancy. The 128 tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\cite{yang2024qwen2} that generates continuous 7-DoF actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters) uses DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders with a two-layer MLP projector that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone, producing 256 tokens that preserve per-token independence.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

We evaluate on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks with three suites: Object (10 manipulation tasks), Spatial (10 spatial reasoning tasks), and Goal (10 goal-conditioned tasks). Each condition uses 50 episodes per task (500 per suite), evaluated by closed-loop Success Rate (SR) with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals. SmolVLA baselines: 44.8\% (Object), 51.8\% (Spatial), 63.0\% (Goal); OpenVLA baseline: 74.0\% (Object).

\subsection{SmolVLA Pruning Ablation}
\label{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}

We train an Attention-weighted Cumulative Importance Score (ACIS) module (3.46M parameters, 0.77\% of SmolVLA) to predict per-token importance scores. Training uses a Stage-1-only protocol: ACIS parameters are optimized while all VLA weights remain frozen, ensuring the scorer identifies important tokens under the original representation. The ablation varies three axes: \emph{scoring} (learned ACIS vs.\ random), \emph{retention ratio} $k \in \{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$, and \emph{mechanism} (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}), yielding seven configurations plus the unpruned baseline.

\subsection{OpenVLA Cross-Architecture Evaluation}
\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO-Object under random token removal (true removal) at $k \in \{0.30, 0.50, 0.70\}$, retaining 30\%--70\% of 256 vision tokens. No learned scorer is used; the comparison isolates the vision connector's effect under the simplest pruning strategy.

\subsection{Smart Compress: Learned Compression}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

To test whether learned compression can bypass the cliff-edge, we insert a cross-attention pooling module between SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector and LLM backbone. The module consists of $M$ learned query vectors that attend over all 128 source tokens through multi-head cross-attention (8 heads, 960 dimensions), producing $M$ compressed representations. The compressor adds 22.2M parameters ($\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) and is trained end-to-end on LIBERO-Object for 100K steps with the vision encoder frozen. We evaluate $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$, where $M = 128$ is a passthrough control. Training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

\section{Analysis and Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Connector Design Determines Compressibility}
\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}

The divergence between SmolVLA and OpenVLA traces to their vision connectors. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle applies $4 \times 4$ spatial folding, so each of its 128 tokens encodes 16 patches via channel concatenation. Removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field with no redundancy from neighbors (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}). Because the folding is fixed and non-learned, no downstream layer can recover discarded information. OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently: removing one of 256 tokens discards $\sim$1/256 of the visual field, a localized loss that self-attention layers can partially compensate. At $k{=}0.50$, OpenVLA retains 37.4\% SR against a 74.0\% baseline; SmolVLA collapses to 0\% even at $k{=}0.95$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pixel_shuffle.pdf}
\caption{Pixel-shuffle folding: each $4{\times}4$ block of SigLIP patches is channel-concatenated into one token. Removing any token eliminates its receptive field (gray) with no redundancy.}
\label{fig:pixel_shuffle}
\end{figure}

The attention entropy ratio quantifies this structural difference. We measure normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights on vision tokens (1.0 = uniform, lower = selective). SmolVLA's ratio is 0.95, near-uniform across all 16 layers (range 0.93--0.98, total variation 0.05). OpenVLA's overall ratio is 0.64, with progressive sharpening from 0.98 in layer 0 to 0.48 in layer 29 (total variation 0.50, $10\times$ SmolVLA's; see Figure~\ref{fig:entropy_layers} and Appendix~\ref{app:entropy_details}). This reflects fundamentally different information structures: pixel-shuffle tokens are uniformly information-dense and resist selective attention at every processing depth, while MLP-projected tokens allow the model to learn spatial selectivity starting from early layers. The entropy ratio may serve as a preliminary compressibility signal, though validation on additional connector types (Q-Former, Perceiver, linear projection) is needed.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_entropy_layers.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer attention entropy ratio. SmolVLA (16 layers): range 0.93--0.98. OpenVLA (32 layers): progressive sharpening from 0.98 to 0.48. The $10\times$ difference in cross-layer variation reflects the connector's effect on token granularity.}
\label{fig:entropy_layers}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Why Learned Compression Succeeds}
\label{sec:learned_compression}

Hard pruning applies a binary mask: selected tokens are kept, the rest discarded. Cross-attention pooling operates differently: each of $M$ queries computes a weighted sum over all 128 source tokens. Even at $M = 32$ (75\% reduction), every original token contributes through its attention weight; no information is deleted, only re-encoded. The $M = 128$ passthrough control confirms that the $\sim$15-point gain over baseline originates from the re-encoding itself: the 22.2M-parameter cross-attention module introduces learned, task-adapted capacity that breaks the rigid spatial entanglement of pixel-shuffle tokens. Two factors explain the benefit: the module can emphasize task-relevant spatial features during training, and each query attends across the original $4 \times 4$ receptive field boundaries, integrating information that the pixel-shuffle connector keeps separated.

\subsection{Disentangling Connector from Scale}
\label{sec:scale_confound}

Our comparison confounds the vision connector with model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B). Four observations favor the connector-level explanation: (1) the official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2\% baseline) shows the same cliff-edge, ruling out undertrained weights; (2) collapse occurs at $k = 0.95$ (removing only 7 of 128 tokens), a perturbation too small for a capacity explanation; (3) the entropy ratio difference (0.98 vs.\ 0.64) is an architectural measurement, not capacity-dependent; (4) pixel-shuffle's $4 \times 4$ folding provides a mechanistic account of why each token is indispensable. External evidence from EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} (CogACT retains 97.8\% SR under 78\% MLP-projector pruning) supports this pattern across three models and two connector families. Controlled matched-scale comparisons remain future work.

\subsection{Offline Metrics Do Not Predict Closed-Loop Failure}
\label{sec:metric_disconnect}

Offline evaluation metrics (importance margins, per-step action loss) provide misleadingly positive signals during pruning. The gap arises because offline metrics average over independent timesteps, while closed-loop execution compounds errors: a single corrupted action pushes the robot off-trajectory, subsequent observations diverge from training data, and recovery becomes impossible. In our single-step analysis, pruning produces 111.5\% relative action error with complete gripper inversion (Table~\ref{tab:offline_action_error}, Appendix~\ref{app:offline_error}). These failures are rare enough to be averaged away in offline metrics but fatal in any episode where they occur. Closed-loop evaluation is a necessary validation step for any VLA compression method.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our evaluation uses the LIBERO simulation benchmark, which does not capture real-world deployment conditions (sensor noise, calibration drift, physical contact dynamics). We compare two architectures that differ in both connector design and scale; controlled comparisons at matched scale remain future work. Smart Compress is evaluated on 10 LIBERO-Object tasks; its generalization to longer-horizon or cross-embodiment settings is untested. Inference cost analysis (latency, KV-cache) is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:cost_benefit}.

