\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We evaluate token pruning across two VLA architectures on LIBERO-Object~\cite{liu2023libero} (10 tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total). All success rates include Wilson 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{SmolVLA: Cliff-Edge Under All Pruning Methods}
\label{sec:smolvla_results}

The SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode, all 128 vision tokens) achieves 44.8\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\%, 49.2\%]).\footnote{The official SmolVLA result is 87.3\% SR with batch size 64 on 8 GPUs. Our reproduction uses a smaller batch due to compute constraints.} Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation} reports the full ablation. Every pruning configuration produces 0\% SR (Wilson CI upper bounds $\leq$1.26\%). Three observations make this striking: (1)~\textbf{Scoring-independent}: random selection performs identically to learned ACIS scoring. (2)~\textbf{Ratio-independent}: retaining 95\% of tokens ($k{=}0.95$, removing only 7 of 128) causes the same failure as retaining 50\%. (3)~\textbf{Mechanism-independent}: zero-masking, true removal, and token merging all produce 0\% SR.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{SmolVLA pruning ablation on LIBERO-Object. All configurations collapse to 0\% SR. The official checkpoint (59.2\% baseline) shows the same cliff-edge.}
\label{tab:smolvla_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{llccrr}
\toprule
Scoring & $k$ & Mechanism & SR (\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\
\midrule
ACIS & 0.50 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 300 & [0, 1.26] \\
ACIS & 0.70 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
ACIS & 0.90 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
Random & 0.95 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
Random & 0.50 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
ACIS & 0.50 & True removal & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.80] \\
ACIS & 0.50 & Merge (ToMe) & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\textit{Official checkpoint (HuggingFaceVLA/smolvla\_libero)}} \\
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 59.2 & 500 & [54.8, 63.4] \\
Random & 0.50 & Zero-mask & 0.00 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2\% SR, CI [54.8\%, 63.4\%]) exhibits identical failure under 50\% random pruning (0/500, 0.00\% SR), confirming the cliff-edge is architectural. Cross-suite evaluation on LIBERO-Spatial (51.8\% $\to$ 0\%) and LIBERO-Goal (63.0\% $\to$ 0\%) confirms the pattern is not task-specific. A geometric prior (Sobel edge scoring) at $k \in \{0.50, 0.70, 0.90\}$ also produces 0\% SR.\footnote{The geometric prior uses a different checkpoint; we report it separately. The qualitative conclusion is identical.}

\subsection{OpenVLA: Graceful Degradation}
\label{sec:openvla_results}

Under random token removal, OpenVLA degrades proportionally rather than collapsing: 49.0\% SR at $k{=}0.70$ (33.8\% relative drop), 37.4\% at $k{=}0.50$ (49.5\%), and 20.6\% at $k{=}0.30$ (72.2\%). The absolute degradation is approximately linear at $\sim$7.6 percentage points per 10\% of tokens removed (Figure~\ref{fig:results_combined}a). At $k{=}0.30$, two tasks (cream\_cheese, butter) reach 0\% SR, but eight retain non-zero performance, confirming graded rather than cliff-edge degradation. Per-task breakdowns are in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}.

The divergence between architectures under identical pruning (random, $k{=}0.50$, true removal) is stark: SmolVLA drops from 44.8\% to 0\%; OpenVLA drops from 74.0\% to 37.4\%. Both models see the same visual input on the same benchmark; they differ in the vision connector (pixel-shuffle vs.\ MLP projector) and model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B). Section~\ref{sec:analysis} analyzes this divergence.

\subsection{Smart Compress: Learned Compression Overcomes the Cliff-Edge}
\label{sec:smart_compress_results}

Both Smart Compress and the baseline start from the same pretrained checkpoint (SmolVLA-Base) and train for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters; the only architectural difference is the 22.2M-parameter cross-attention compressor (Smart Compress trains 122.1M total parameters including the expert action head, vs.\ 99.9M for the baseline).

Table~\ref{tab:smart_compress} reports all five configurations. The cross-attention module improves performance regardless of compression: $M \in \{32, 64, 128\}$ all achieve $\sim$60\% SR, exceeding the 44.8\% baseline by 15--17 points ($p < 0.001$, two-proportion $z$-test). The $M = 128$ passthrough (59.8\% SR, no compression) matches $M = 32$ (61.6\%, 75\% reduction), establishing that the gain originates from re-encoding, not compression. Only at $M = 16$ (87.5\% compression) does performance decline modestly to 55.8\%, still 11 points above baseline. $M = 96$ (52.6\%) falls below trend and likely reflects training dynamics. In the same setting where every hard pruning method produces 0\%, the worst Smart Compress configuration exceeds the uncompressed baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:results_combined}b). Per-task results are in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Smart Compress on LIBERO-Object. Performance is flat across $M \in \{32, 64, 128\}$ ($\sim$60\% SR), showing the gain comes from re-encoding, not compression. $M{=}16$ shows modest decline; $M{=}96$ is an outlier.}
\label{tab:smart_compress}
\begin{tabular}{lcccrr}
\toprule
Method & $M$ & Reduction & SR (\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\textit{Baseline} & 128 & 0\% & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\
\textit{Hard pruning} & -- & 50--90\% & 0.00 & 500+ & [0, 0.76] \\
\midrule
Smart Compress & 128 & 0\% & 59.8 & 500 & [55.4, 64.0] \\
Smart Compress & 96 & 25\% & 52.6 & 500 & [48.2, 56.9] \\
Smart Compress & 64 & 50\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\
Smart Compress & 32 & 75\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\
Smart Compress & 16 & 87.5\% & 55.8 & 500 & [51.4, 60.1] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_degradation_curve.pdf}
\caption{Token pruning: SmolVLA (cliff-edge) vs.\ OpenVLA (graded).}
\label{fig:degradation_curve}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_compression_curve.pdf}
\caption{Smart Compress: re-encoding exceeds baseline at all levels.}
\label{fig:compression_curve}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Architecture-dependent response to token reduction. (a) SmolVLA collapses to 0\% at every pruning ratio tested ($k \in \{0.50\text{--}0.95\}$); OpenVLA degrades approximately linearly. The official SmolVLA checkpoint (dashed) shows the same cliff-edge. (b) Learned cross-attention compression raises SR to $\sim$60\% regardless of output token count $M$, exceeding the uncompressed baseline (dashed) at every configuration.}
\label{fig:results_combined}
\end{figure}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Vision token efficiency.}
Token pruning in ViTs is well established: DynamicViT~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}, EViT~\cite{liang2022evit}, and ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome} consistently remove 30--60\% of tokens with $<$1\% accuracy loss on ImageNet. For VLMs, FastV~\cite{chen2024fastv} and LLaVA-PruMerge~\cite{shang2024prumerge} extend pruning to language-vision tasks. We test whether these results transfer to closed-loop VLA control, where action errors compound across timesteps.

\paragraph{VLA-specific token reduction.}
VLA-Cache~\cite{xu2025vlacache} caches vision KV states across timesteps for 1.7$\times$ speedup without removing tokens. VLA-IAP~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap} uses interaction-aligned pruning on OpenVLA; EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} combines layer pruning and token selection on CogACT; VLA-Pruner~\cite{liu2025vlapruner} uses dual-level importance criteria for temporal-aware pruning. All evaluate on MLP-projector architectures. None reports the cliff-edge failure we observe on pixel-shuffle VLAs, suggesting their positive results may not generalize to spatially entangled connectors.

\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
Per-patch MLP projectors~\cite{liu2023llava,kim2024openvla} preserve per-token granularity. Cross-attention connectors (Q-Former~\cite{li2023blip2}, Perceiver~\cite{alayrac2022flamingo,jaegle2021perceiver}) compress to learned queries at the architectural level. Pixel-shuffle connectors~\cite{shi2016subpixel,shukor2025smolvla,chen2024internvl} fold patches via channel concatenation, achieving training-time compression but creating inter-token dependencies. Our analysis links this taxonomy to inference-time compressibility and shows that cross-attention re-encoding can overcome the failure mode of deletion on pixel-shuffle architectures.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first cross-architecture closed-loop evaluation of vision token reduction in VLA models. Compressibility is determined by the vision connector: pixel-shuffle connectors create spatially entangled tokens that collapse under any form of deletion (59.2\% $\to$ 0\% on the official checkpoint), while MLP projectors degrade gracefully (74.0\% $\to$ 37.4\%). The attention entropy ratio (0.98 vs.\ 0.64) captures this divergence, consistent across three models and two connector families~\cite{yang2025efficientvla}. For architectures where pruning fails, cross-attention re-encoding raises SR from 44.8\% to $\sim$60\%, and compressing to 32 queries (75\% token reduction) preserves this gain at 61.6\%, demonstrating that re-encoding can succeed where deletion cannot. Standard offline metrics do not predict these closed-loop outcomes; closed-loop evaluation is essential for validating VLA compression.

tool_args.new_text:
\paragraph{Measurement conditions.} Both models are measured on real LIBERO rollout data: SmolVLA on 2400 frames (overall entropy) and 260 frames across all 10 LIBERO-Object tasks (per-layer analysis), OpenVLA on 250 frames from 50 episodes $\times$ 5 initial frames per episode. The sample size difference reflects computational constraints (OpenVLA's 7B forward pass is substantially more expensive), but both measurements use task-relevant visual inputs rather than random noise. For OpenVLA, the entropy statistics show low variance across frames (std $< 0.02$ for the per-layer entropy ratio), suggesting that 250 frames are sufficient for convergence.


\section{Inference Cost-Benefit Analysis}
\label{app:cost_benefit}

The Smart Compress module reduces token count from 128 to 32 (75\%), but end-to-end inference latency improves modestly: 154.78\,ms at $M{=}32$ versus 164.33\,ms at full token count, a $1.06\times$ speedup (6\% latency reduction). At $M{=}64$: 156.16\,ms ($1.05\times$). The gap between token reduction and latency gain reflects SmolVLA's compact size (451.5M parameters): self-attention over 128 vision tokens is not the inference bottleneck, and the compressor's 22.2M-parameter cross-attention computation partially offsets downstream savings.

The primary benefit at this model scale is memory: reducing from 128 to 32 vision tokens shrinks the KV-cache footprint for the vision portion by 75\%, a meaningful reduction for memory-constrained robotic hardware. For larger VLA models (7B+ parameters), where attention cost scales quadratically with sequence length, the same 75\% token reduction would yield substantially larger latency gains. Whether the training cost (100K steps, 6.5 hours per configuration) is justified depends on deployment context: for VLAs deployed across many tasks, the one-time training cost is amortized; for rapid prototyping, retraining is a barrier.

\paragraph{Comparison with the official checkpoint.}
Smart Compress at $M = 128$ (59.8\%, CI [55.4\%, 64.0\%]) achieves performance comparable to the official SmolVLA checkpoint trained with 16$\times$ larger batch size (59.2\%, CI [54.8\%, 63.4\%]). The overlapping confidence intervals preclude strong conclusions about the equivalence of these mechanisms. This comparison should be interpreted cautiously given the different training conditions (batch size, total compute, optimizer state).

